      "math_academy_id": "q-141286",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
